  "passage": "…that one of the company’s biggest upcoming products is running late. Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Kyber, Nvidia’s next rack-scale server design, has been pushed back more than a year , from 2027 to 2028.\nKyber matters because it’s built to pack 144 of Nvidia’s most powerful chips into a single cabinet, letting them work together like one giant computer. That kind of processing power is what AI companies need to train and run their most advanced models.\nThe design was supposed to launch alongside Vera Rubin Ultra, Nvidia’s next-generation system, in 2027. The holdup comes down to manufacturing problems with a specialized circuit board inside the system, according to SemiAnalysis.\nA larger version, called NVL576, which links eight racks together via optical connections, is also likely to be delayed or to ship only in small volumes.\nNvidia had a backup plan: bolting two current-generation racks together to achieve similar power, but that idea was scrapped too.\nCloud customers reportedly rejected it because the design was awkward and the service was expensive to run. SemiAnalysis says this leaves Nvidia without a proven way to scale up Rubin Ultra for now, which could give rivals like AMD and Google an opening at the high end of the AI chip market. Nvidia has not responded to requests for comment.\nDespite this news, Nvidia stock isn’t showing much reaction, trading close to flat. That’s likely because the company’s current-generation Rubin systems are already in full production and set to start shipping this fall to eig…",
  "statement": "A larger version, called NVL576, which links eight racks together via optical connections, is also likely to be delayed or to ship only in small volumes."
 },
 {
  "id": "G406",
  "article_id": "gnews:297518a86b6ac724cee70e00",
  "title": "Gradium Raises $100M Seed With NVIDIA Backing",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-10",
  "article_opening": "CMSWire's Marketing & Customer Experience Leadership channel is the go-to hub for actionable research, editorial and opinion for CMOs, aspiring CMOs and today's customer experience innovators. Our dedicated editorial and research teams focus on bringing you the data and information you need to navigate today's complex customer, organizational and technical landscapes. We've got you covered.\nBy Michelle Hawley\nThe Paris-based voice AI startup extended its seed round seven months after launch and ",
  "passage": "…ou the data and information you need to navigate today's complex customer, organizational and technical landscapes. We've got you covered.\nBy Michelle Hawley\nThe Paris-based voice AI startup extended its seed round seven months after launch and will open a San Francisco Bay Area office.\nFunding milestone reached . Gradium raises total funding to $100 million.\nSilicon Valley expansion . New office in San Francisco Bay to boost AI presence.\nEnterprise developer impact . Increased investment enables faster innovation for enterprise voice AI infrastructure.\nGradium has extended its seed financing to $100 million, seven months after launch. The extension added new investors including NVIDIA, and will fund a new San Francisco Bay Area office.\nThe company was founded in September 2025 by researchers behind Kyutai, an AI research lab known for real-time speech systems. Co-founders Neil Zeghidour, Laurent Mazaré, Olivier Teboul and Alexandre Défossez built Gradium to move frontier voice AI research into production.\nAccording to company officials , the capital will accelerate AI research, product development and international expansion. Gradium said it generated revenue within weeks of launch, though it did not disclose figures.\n\"Surpassing $100 million in funding and expanding our investors marks an important milestone for Gradium. It enables us to accelerate our roadmap, expand our Bay Area presence, and bring years of breakthrou…",
  "statement": "will fund a new San Francisco Bay Area office"
 },
 {
  "id": "G432",
  "article_id": "gnews:57de52b4f33b0c903d256f9e",
  "title": "DeepSeek Just Dealt Nvidia Stock a Potentially Major Blow",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-09",
  "article_opening": "DeepSeek has already rattled Nvidia Corporation (NVDA) investors once with its low-cost artificial intelligence (AI) models. Now, the Chinese startup may be posing a far more direct competitive threat. Reports that DeepSeek is developing its own inference-focused AI chip could reduce its reliance on Nvidia's GPUs, highlighting a broader industry trend in which leading AI developers are increasingly designing custom silicon to lower costs, improve efficiency, and gain greater control over their A",
  "passage": "…entum has eased in recent months. Despite continuing to deliver strong operating performance, the stock is up just 9.45% year-to-date (YTD) and 27.57% over the past 52 weeks, lagging the broader technology sector.\nThe stock is currently trading 13.7% below its 52-week high of $236.54 reached on May 14. Investor sentiment has been pressured by concerns over the sustainability of AI infrastructure spending, intensifying competition, U.S. export restrictions, and uncertainty surrounding the rollout of next-generation AI platforms, including Rubin and Kyber.\nRecently, Nvidia faced reports that suggested its next-generation Kyber rack-scale AI infrastructure could be delayed until 2028. However, the company denied the speculation, maintaining that its roadmap remains on track. Designed for the Rubin Ultra platform, Kyber is expected to launch in 2027 as an integrated AI infrastructure system and a key component of Nvidia's long-term AI infrastructure strategy.\nNvidia trades at 22.49 times forward earnings, which is currently a discount compared to industry peers and the historical average.\nNvidia delivered another exceptional quarter when it reported first-quarter fiscal 2027 results on May 20, further cementing its leadership in the rapidly growing AI infrastructure market.\nFor the quarter ended April 26, Nvidia posted record revenue of $81.6 billion, an 85% year-over-year (YOY) increase, while net income jumped 211% YOY to $58.32 billion. On a non-GAAP basis, …",
  "statement": "the company denied the speculation, maintaining that its roadmap remains on track"
 },
 {
  "id": "G220",
  "article_id": "gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6",
  "title": "Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "Up until now the majority of AI buildouts have been primarily cashflow funded by the hyperscalers such as Google, Amazon, Meta, Microsoft, Oracle. Over the last year, that's started to turn with Oracle then Meta, and now even Google turning to debt. Nvidia revenue is skyrocketing, and even 3 years into the build out, the general market is still materially lower on shipment volumes and revenue estimates for Nvidia in the 2nd half of this year versus our through supply chain tracking in the Accele",
  "passage": "…hat's started to turn with Oracle then Meta, and now even Google turning to debt. Nvidia revenue is skyrocketing, and even 3 years into the build out, the general market is still materially lower on shipment volumes and revenue estimates for Nvidia in the 2nd half of this year versus our through supply chain tracking in the Accelerator Model .\nAI Debt Financing will become a multi-trillion-dollar credit market, with over $7T of debt outstanding by 2029 driven both by AI IT Capex and AI Datacenter Capex needs for Neoclouds, datacenter builders, and even hyperscalers. This will make it the second largest asset backed debt market after the US mortgage-backed financing market at just over $13T.\nAnnual AI Capex – including GPUs, networking, storage and attached CPU compute as well as for the Datacenters to house AI compute – will be well north of $2T in 2028. Cumulative AI Capex from 2024 to 2029 will reach ~$11.1T, and credit markets will be the main funding source for this buildout.\nThe surge in borrowing requirements to date has driven by briskly growing demand from AI labs and hyperscalers, and the construction of AI clusters to service this demand was made financeable by long-term take-or-pay compute contracts backed by hyperscalers, with the most common offtake period being 5 years. Though this article will mainly focus on financing AI IT Capex - that is, GPUs and related capex like storage, networking and CPUs attached - there is much that also needs to be done to support the growth of datacenter capex financing. A much more detailed bre…",
  "statement": "Annual AI Capex – including GPUs, networking, storage and attached CPU compute as well as for the Datacenters to house AI compute – will be well north of $2T in 2028"
 },
 {
  "id": "G483",
  "article_id": "gnews:eeaf70fb8a11065d200d772a",
  "title": "Why Nvidia-Backed Nebius Group Stock Skyrocketed Thursday Morning",
  "publication_day": "2026-09-17",
  "article_opening": "Nebius Group ( NBIS +1.39% ) stock surged sharply higher Thursday morning, gaining as much as 11.2%. As of 2:27 p.m. ET, the stock had given back most of those gains, but was still up 2.1%.\nThe catalyst that sent the artificial intelligence (AI) and neocloud specialist higher was a report that the company is raising some prices.\nNebius is one of several neocloud operators that stockpile graphics processing units (GPUs) and provide on-demand AI processing. The company announced that it will raise",
  "passage": "…at demand.\"\nNebius is recognized by Nvidia as a Reference Platform Cloud Partner, a designation reserved for select cloud providers that operate large-scale AI clusters optimized with Nvidia's reference architecture. Moreover, the chipmaker holds a 9.3% stake in Nebius, which includes a $2 billion investment announced earlier this year. The partnership also gives Nebius early access to Nvidia's top-of-the-line AI processors.\nThe accelerating demand is clear in the company's results. In the second quarter, Nebius grew revenue 454% year over year to $582 million. While heavy capital expenditures weighed on the bottom line, the company still slashed its adjusted net loss by 64% to $33 million. Management expects full-year revenue of $3.2 billion and an annualized run rate of $8 billion, both at the midpoint of its guidance.\nNebius has plenty of growth baked in, as the stock is selling for 43 times sales, which is a premium to be sure. However, if the company can continue its triple-digit growth, the combination of scale and leverage will help Nebius turn a profit, likely sooner than later.\nBy Danny Vena, CPA – Sep 17, 2026 at 2:31PM EST",
  "statement": "Management expects full-year revenue of $3.2 billion and an annualized run rate of $8 billion, both at the midpoint of its guidance."
 },
 {
  "id": "G041",
  "article_id": "gnews:26325b9e051efe16db34f758",
  "title": "Verkada Accelerates Physical AI with NVIDIA",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-01",
  "article_opening": "Verkada is scaling its physical AI platform across more than 2.4 million devices globally with a new technical collaboration and investment from NVIDIA\nSAN MATEO, Calif. , July 1, 2026 /PRNewswire/ -- Verkada , a leader in AI-powered physical security and operations, today announced a collaboration with NVIDIA to accelerate the development and deployment of physical AI across the built environment. NVIDIA also joins as a new investor in Verkada, following a strategic investment from Alphabet's C",
  "passage": "Verkada is scaling its physical AI platform across more than 2.4 million devices globally with a new technical collaboration and investment from NVIDIA\nSAN MATEO, Calif. , July 1, 2026 /PRNewswire/ -- Verkada , a leader in AI-powered physical security and operations, today announced a collaboration with NVIDIA to accelerate the development and deployment of physical AI across the built environment. NVIDIA also joins as a new investor in Verkada, following a strategic investment from Alphabet's CapitalG at the end of last year.\nVerkada is applying AI to transform how schools, hospitals, retailers, manufacturers, and other organizations turn real-world operational data into actionable intelligence — helping keep people and places safe.\nVerkada is scaling its physical AI platform across more than 2.4 million devices globally with a new technical collaboration and investment from NVIDIA.\n\"Verkada has been building and deploying Physical AI before the term existed. With our footprint of more than 2.4 million devices across 170 countries and 30,000 organizations, we've proven that the built environment is one of the largest beneficiarie…",
  "statement": "NVIDIA also joins as a new investor in Verkada"
 },
 {
  "id": "G420",
  "article_id": "gnews:6b89157155648b9ec317d209",
  "title": "SambaNova hits $11 billion valuation as investors back Nvidia chip challengers",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-08",
  "article_opening": "An AI chip startup has raised $1 billion in financing as investors continue to pour money into companies looking to challenge Nvidia .\nSambaNova is now valued at $11 billion thanks to the financing, which was led by General Atlantic along with participation from Seligman Ventures, T. Rowe Price and Capital Group.\nThe latest round of funding, announced Wednesday, comes after the startup raised more than $350 million earlier this year from investors including Intel , with which it also announced a",
  "passage": "…igned to run large AI models in a quick and cost-efficient manner, which has become a particular focus for the industry as more complex AI agents are deployed.\nThe company sells its latest chip — the SN50 — as part of a server unit that can be deployed in data centers. This is different to the graphics processing unit (GPU) architecture that Nvidia has sold and has been critical for training huge AI models.\nBut SambaNova is also focused on so-called on-premise deployments where its server units can be installed at a data center owned by a specific company. On Wednesday, JPMorgan Chase said it would deploy SambaNova's systems for \"on-prem inference in our demanding enterprise AI workloads.\"\n\"JPMorgan has selected SambaNova to be the inference provider for the bank,\" Liang said.\n\"For banks and for other industries where data is incredibly important, bringing this infrastructure on prem, bringing this infrastructure with models that are then under your control with your private data, and having all within your firewalls is an incredibly important aspect of running AI in a very secure and private manner,\" he added.\nSambaNova has said that on-premises inference can mean faster, more secure AI as it's controlled by the company using it rather than a third-party cloud provider or AI lab.\nPublic market investors have been bullish on the semiconductor sector, which is often dubbed the \"picks and shovels\" of the AI buildout. The PHLX semiconductor index , whi…",
  "statement": "JPMorgan has selected SambaNova to be the inference provider for the bank"
 },
 {
  "id": "G145",
  "article_id": "gnews:068a24e2743bab750f43f048",
  "title": "Nvidia vs. Strategy: Which Stock Has a Better Chance of Surging 10x by 2036?",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-04",
  "article_opening": "Nvidia ( NVDA +2.48% ) needs no introduction. It's at the center of the artificial intelligence (AI) boom, supplying industry-leading hardware and software for the infrastructure build-out.\nInvestors might have heard of Strategy ( MSTR -3.06% ) . It's a $32 billion enterprise software business that morphed into a Bitcoin treasury company.\nBetween these two stocks, whose recent performances are on opposite ends of the spectrum, which has a better chance of surging 10x by 2036?\nNvidia's incredible",
  "passage": "…aler customers race to build computing capacity to meet their clients' needs.\nPhenomenal top-line growth has boosted the company's profit. Net income totaled $58.3 billion in the most recent fiscal quarter. That metric was just $2 billion exactly three years before, translating to a monster 29-fold three-year rise. Nvidia's pricing power plays a role here, as its gross margin was 74.9% during Q1.\nNvidia has a wide economic moat. The company clearly excels in hardware, but its CUDA software platform raises switching costs for developers. The combination of chips and dedicated software creates a robust ecosystem.\nYou're forgiven if you think this AI stock is extremely expensive. But it's not. Shares trade at a price-to-earnings ratio of 30.6. Considering Nvidia's unbelievable revenue and profit growth, this is an attractive entry point for interested buyers.\nStrategy continues to fine-tune its strategy\nStrategy is the single largest holder of Bitcoin in the world. As of June 30, the business held 847,363 units of the leading cryptocurrency on its balance sheet. This is a Bitcoin treasury company that provides leveraged exposure to the digital asset for its shareholders. So, the stock price is more volatile than the crypto. Strategy shares trade 82% below their peak, while Bitcoin is 52% off its record.\nIn a bull market, though, the company's stock is poised to be a monster winner. The digital asset reached an all-time high in O…",
  "statement": "Shares trade at a price-to-earnings ratio of 30.6"
 },
 {
  "id": "G206",
  "article_id": "nvblog:open-models-icml-2026",
  "title": "How Open Models Are Driving AI Research | NVIDIA Blog",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "At the International Conference on Machine Learning, nearly 145 papers cite NVIDIA Nemotron open models and datasets as the foundation for new research.\nEvery year, the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML) reveals where thousands of AI researchers have decided to put their work.\nThis year’s accepted papers reveal a clear direction: open frontier models and open AI infrastructure have become foundational to how modern AI science gets done.\nNVIDIA had 74 papers accepted at ICML 2026",
  "passage": "…eyond NVIDIA’s own research labs .\nBasecamp Research developed a new DNA foundation model, EDEN , that helps researchers interpret and design genetic sequences.\nMerck & Co. , uses KERMT to predict how potential drug molecules may behave in the body, including whether they are likely to be effective, safe and developable.\nSakana AI — attending ICML this year — built its Fugu and Fugu-Ultra models directly on Nemotron 3 Ultra, using the open foundation to push forward its work on AI research automation.\nKiloCode integrated Nemotron into its code-routing architecture, reporting token cost reductions of up to 90% — a result with real implications for the economics of deploying AI in production.\nNAVER developed its own model using the Nemotron architecture, extending the foundation for Korean-language AI research.\nTogether AI is hosting Nemotron models on its platform, making them more accessible to researchers who need reliable, seamless access to open inference.\nHumanoid , LG Electronics , NEURA Robotics and Noble Machines are adopting NVIDIA Isaac GR00T models to accelerate industrial deployments of their humanoids, while 1X , Agility , Agile Robots , Boston Dynamics , Hexagon Robotics , and Mentee are building the next generation of humanoids using Cosmos world models, Isaac Sim and Isaac Lab to accelerate the development and validation of their robots.\nExplore NVIDIA’s open models on Hugging Face .\nExplore genomics and biology research a…",
  "statement": "NAVER developed its own model using the Nemotron architecture"
 },
 {
  "id": "G167",
  "article_id": "gnews:2f953a56cd06571ab0afc348",
  "title": "AMD Stock and Intel Crushed Nvidia in the First Half. Here's My Prediction for the Second Half.",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-05",
  "article_opening": "Nvidia ( NVDA +2.48% ) has been the \"go-to\" artificial intelligence (AI) stock for a number of years. Since this tech giant is the No. 1 designer of AI chips, it's generated mind-boggling revenue growth that's reached record levels and proven itself to be a winner in the AI boom. So it's no surprise that investors have turned to the stock, pushing it to a gain of about 900% over the past five years.\nBut in the first half of this year, a shift took place. Investors rotated out of some of the bigg",
  "passage": "…he second half. I predict that Nvidia will outpace these rivals. And this is for two reasons: Nvidia's next goal and its valuation. I'll start with the goal, and this is to dominate the $200 billion CPU market -- a market where Nvidia has not been greatly present in the past. In fact, it's launching its first-ever stand-alone CPU this fall as part of the Vera Rubin platform. And at the same time, Nvidia is targeting the personal computing market with a superchip -- also set for fall release -- that includes a GPU and a CPU.\nWhile it may be difficult for Nvidia to take complete leadership of the entire CPU market, I think the company could clearly dominate in the CPU market for data centers. Nvidia already forecasts $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales this year. It's important to note that the CPU is the key chip needed to power agentic AI -- and agentic AI is expected to be the next AI growth driver. This involves the application of AI to real-world problems, with the agent taking problem-solving steps.\nWhile Nvidia's potential CPU market leadership won't happen overnight, a successful launch of the Rubin platform and high demand could lead to positive stock performance for Nvidia in the second half.\nValuation could also push investors to favor Nvidia over AMD and Intel.\nAt about 22x forward earnings estimates, Nvidia looks dirt cheap, while the two chip peers look excessively expensive after their recent gains. All of this prompts me to predict …",
  "statement": "Nvidia already forecasts $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales this year"
 },
 {
  "id": "G352",
  "article_id": "gnews:9026c944c63575a080ce7ba9",
  "title": "A $10,000 Investment in Nvidia at the Start of 2026 Is Up Only 5% -- Here's One Promising Sector That Could Reignite Excitement",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-07",
  "article_opening": "For the first six months of 2026, Nvidia ( NVDA +2.56% ) hasn't led the artificial intelligence (AI) trade. It opened the year at $189.84 on Jan. 2 and closed at around $200 on June 30. That's a roughly 5% gain.\nAnyone who invested $10,000 to start the year hasn't seen much progress in their Nvidia investment. Still, there's a market sector that can open up new revenue opportunities and reward patient Nvidia shareholders.\nWhat a $10,000 investment is worth after the first half of 2026\nIn the las",
  "passage": "…he chipmaker can still reward long-term shareholders, but expectations should be reasonable. Nvidia is not currently offering the kind of monster gains it has been known for in the past.\nAnyone who purchased $10,000 worth of Nvidia stock through fractional investing at its Jan. 2 opening price of $189.84 got a little more than 52 shares. With the closing price of $200 on June 30, that initial $10,000 investment would be worth roughly $10,534.\nWhat could help reignite enthusiasm around Nvidia?\nOne revenue growth opportunity for Nvidia will be in space, and it has already quietly been a behind-the-scenes player through its involvement with Space Exploration Technologies .\nWhen the AI start-up Anthropic announced it was renting compute capacity from SpaceX's data center, it revealed that the data center is powered by over 220,000 Nvidia graphics processing units (GPUs). Alphabet has a similar deal, renting compute capacity that gives it access to 110,000 Nvidia GPUs.\nThat involvement with SpaceX will expand further, as Nvidia is set to serve as an initial supplier of hardware for SpaceX's orbital data centers. SpaceX's first version of the satellites it plans to use as data centers in space is called AI1 and will use Nvidia chips.\nAlso, Nvidia announced in March that it will be launching its Vera Rubin Space-1 Module at some point in the future. Instead of beaming data back to Earth for processing, this module will run AI computing directly in space.\n\"AI processing across space and ground systems enables real-time sensing, decision-making, and auto…",
  "statement": "Anthropic announced it was renting compute capacity from SpaceX's data center, it revealed that the data center is powered by over 220,000 Nvidia graphics processing units"
 },
 {
  "id": "G460",
  "article_id": "nvblog:performance-per-watt-ai-infrastructure-efficiency",
  "title": "Why Performance per Watt Is the Ultimate Metric for AI Infrastructure Efficiency | NVIDIA Blog",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-14",
  "article_opening": "From benchmark to production, NVIDIA Blackwell NVL72 delivers the highest performance per watt to maximize revenue and the lowest token cost to maximize profit margins.\nPower is AI infrastructure’s inescapable constraint. How many tokens an AI factory can generate within a fixed power budget determines its revenue and profitability. Because of this, performance per watt — a metric that can’t be gamed, only earned through real-world results — is the foundation for AI factories.\nAs agentic AI driv",
  "passage": "…dling them requires engineering rigor and time in production.\nNVIDIA Blackwell NVL72 systems continues to set the standard across a diverse range of models and production use cases delivering sustained performance, rack-level reliability and economics that hold under real traffic day after day.\nThat’s why leading AI labs such as Anthropic, OpenAI and SpaceXAI use NVIDIA Blackwell NVL72 systems to run inference.\nIn addition, a variety of inference service providers and AI natives use the Blackwell platform to deploy open models in production.\nCoreWeave has deployed Kimi K2.6 on NVIDIA GB300 NVL72, combining NVFP4 quantization and EAGLE3 speculative decoding to maximize inference performance.\nPerplexity runs Qwen3 235B and post-trained Qwen3.5-397B-A17B on NVIDIA GB200 NVL72 for its AI agent platform, serving millions of queries daily with the latency and reliability that consumers need.\nFireworks AI deploys GLM 5.2 on the NVIDIA Blackwell platform, enabling production deployments for customers including Cursor and Factory AI.\nThis accumulated production experience, built across generations of frontier models and real-world deployments, is what gives NVIDIA Vera Rubin its head start.\nLearn more about the NVIDIA Vera Rubin platform in this technical blog and find details on the NVIDIA DSX AI factory-scale platform and DSX MaxLPS .",
  "statement": "Perplexity runs Qwen3 235B and post-trained Qwen3.5-397B-A17B on NVIDIA GB200 NVL72 for its AI agent platform, serving millions of queries daily"
 },
 {
  "id": "G393",
  "article_id": "gnews:2957e5c79a365952140c8cce",
  "title": "DeepSeek develops AI chips, challenging Nvidia and Huawei",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-07",
  "article_opening": "DeepSeek develops AI chips, challenging Nvidia and Huawei\nJAKARTA - Chinese artificial intelligence (AI) startup DeepSeek is developing its own AI chips to reduce its reliance on products from Nvidia and Huawei, which have so far been used in the development of the company's AI models.\nAccording to Reuters , citing three sources familiar with the plans, DeepSeek's chips are being designed specifically for inference, the stage where trained AI models generate responses for users.\nUnlike chips use",
  "passage": "…esigned specifically for inference, the stage where trained AI models generate responses for users.\nUnlike chips used for model training, inference chips generally consume less power and are designed to meet the growing demand for AI computing.\nThe move marks a major strategic shift for DeepSeek, which has so far been known for focusing on AI model development rather than the semiconductor business.\nIf successful, the company would join a number of global AI developers that have begun designing their own chips to gain greater control over computing infrastructure while reducing dependence on Nvidia.\nThe news briefly weighed on Nvidia shares, which fell around 1.6% in US pre-market trading.\n\"Nvidia's position in China is effectively at zero and will remain that way. DeepSeek has almost no chance of selling chips outside China unless it gains access to advanced manufacturing technology,\" said analyst Richard Windsor of Radio Free Mobile, adding that the development would not affect the chipmaker.\nDeepSeek attracted global attention in early 2025 after launching a low-cost, high-performance AI model that disrupted the global technology industry.\nThe Hangzhou-based company has so far been better known for focusing on AI model innovation rather than commercialising the technology it develops.\nAmid US restrictions on AI chip exports to China, Huawei has expanded its domestic market share and now controls around half of China's AI chip market, which is valu…",
  "statement": "Nvidia's position in China is effectively at zero and will remain that way"
 },
 {
  "id": "G478",
  "article_id": "gnews:621e1b678a037049c86a70af",
  "title": "Nvidia vs. Micron: Which Is the Better AI Semiconductor Stock to Own for the Next 5 Years?",
  "publication_day": "2026-09-04",
  "article_opening": "Artificial intelligence (AI) infrastructure stocks are still one of the biggest driving forces in the market. While Nvidia ( NVDA +2.53% ) helped lead the early charge, other stocks, like Micron ( MU +4.20% ) , have greatly outperformed the AI leader over the past year.\nLet's dive into both AI stocks to see which one looks set to outperform over the next five years.\nNvidia was the first big AI winner, and the advantages the company established remain in place today. Meanwhile, despite its massiv",
  "passage": "…orean companies SK Hynix and Samsung -- and derives around three-quarters of its revenue from DRAM and a quarter from NAND (flash). AI is driving memory demand, as GPUs are packaged with high bandwidth memory (HBM), a special form of DRAM that helps reduce latency and optimize performance, and AI training data is being stored in enormous solid state drives (SSDs) that use flash memory.\nRival SK Hynix has predicted that the DRAM market will remain supply-constrained through at least 2030, while Micron and its rivals have begun to lock in long-term contracts for up to five years for the first time ever. This should help reduce some of the extreme cyclicality the industry has seen in the past.\nOn a forward P/E basis, Micron looks very cheap, trading at only 6 times analyst estimates for fiscal 2027 (ending August 2027). The question is how long the memory cycle will last and whether there has been enough of a structural shift in the market to keep prices high.\nI think the easy answer here is that Nvidia will outperform Micron over the next five years. Micron is still a cyclical stock until proven otherwise, and five years is a long time for a cycle to last. Meanwhile, Nvidia has built one of the most complete and powerful AI infrastructure companies on the planet, and the stock is very attractively valued given its growth and moat.\nBy Geoffrey Seiler – Sep 4, 2026 at 1:00AM EST\nMicron has been riding the memory supercycle.",
  "statement": "On a forward P/E basis, Micron looks very cheap, trading at only 6 times analyst estimates for fiscal 2027 (ending August 2027)."
 },
 {
  "id": "G200",
  "article_id": "gnews:f66e1689ec2e27e7eab2a2dd",
  "title": "Nvidia Vera Rubin rack costs $7.8M — memory is why",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "Nvidia's Next AI Rack Costs Nearly Double the Last One, and Memory Is Why. What Infrastructure Buyers Should Budget For.\nNvidia's next-generation Vera Rubin rack will cost hyperscalers $7.8 million—nearly double the prior generation—with memory now accounting for 25-30% of total cost instead of 5-10%. This shift signals that AI infrastructure budgeting must focus on total system cost rather than GPU pricing alone.\nThis story was produced through MarketScale . See how Software & Technology teams ",
  "passage": "…n Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation. The GPUs themselves are not the driver of the increase. The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\nThe memory line is where the cost concentrates. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems. First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\nAs a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\nOnly a handful of companies buy racks at this scale. But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market. Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products. The AI buildout and the price of the memory in a laptop are now tied to the same supply squeeze.\nFor enterprise technology buyer…",
  "statement": "First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth."
 },
 {
  "id": "G054",
  "article_id": "gnews:dde08c03c8dd6f66031632d5",
  "title": "Nvidia launches revenue-sharing model for AI startups",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-02",
  "article_opening": "The chipmaker will earn a share of cloud revenue from partners, with two data center operators named as its first participants\nNvidia $NVDA announced a revenue-sharing program on Wednesday designed to give AI startups, model builders, and enterprises access to its computing infrastructure without requiring them to finance hardware buildouts themselves.\nRevenue from both product sales and cloud operations flows back to Nvidia under the arrangement, as AI cloud companies provide Nvidia -powered se",
  "passage": "…nue from both product sales and cloud operations flows back to Nvidia under the arrangement, as AI cloud companies provide Nvidia -powered services to their end customers. Kress, writing in a blog post, called the resulting income a \"recurring, usage-linked earnings stream\" for Nvidia ; on the customer side, she said the model removes the need to navigate site selection, power procurement, construction, and hardware bring-up before accessing large-scale compute.\nToken credits are also part of the offering, giving startups a way to access compute resources without upfront capital outlays.\nNvidia named two initial partners. Sharon AI will deploy up to 40,000 Nvidia Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs. A campus being developed by Firmus Technologies in Batam, Indonesia is planned at a capacity of up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs and 360 megawatts of power. The company said AI-native firms including Baseten, Fireworks AI, and Together AI represent the kind of customers the program is designed to serve.\n\"This strategic collaboration with Nvidia marks a pivotal moment in Sharon AI's mission to deliver sovereign, large-scale AI compute infrastructure,\" said James Manning, cofounder and CEO of Sharon AI, in a statement.\n\"AI-native companies need access to scalable, energy- and cost-efficient compute infrastructure to compete globally,\" Tim Rosenfield, co-CEO of Firmus Technologies, said in a statement.\nNvidia framed the program as a response to a structural shift in AI demand, from model development toward production inference workloads that requ…",
  "statement": "A campus being developed by Firmus Technologies in Batam, Indonesia is planned at a capacity of up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs and 360 megawatts of power"
 },
 {
  "id": "G253",
  "article_id": "gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32",
  "title": "How Nvidia Became The Black Sheep Of The Chip Stock Rally",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "How Nvidia Became The Black Sheep Of The Chip Stock Rally\nNVIDIA’s fundamentals have never looked stronger, yet its stock is limping through 2026. NVIDIA (NASDAQ:NVDA | NVDA Price Prediction) is up just 3.2% year to date, a striking laggard in a sector where money is flooding into almost everything else.…\nBy Danielle Liverance\nNVDA is up just 7% year to date despite 85% revenue growth to $82B, punished for being priced as the consensus AI trade.\nAMD surged 171% and Micron 305% year to date, rewa",
  "passage": "… CEO Jensen Huang framed it plainly: “Agentic AI has arrived, doing productive work, generating real value and scaling rapidly across companies and industries.” But NVDA trades at a forward P/E of less than 20 now, and when a name becomes the consensus AI trade, even a blowout can underwhelm a market that had already priced perfection. Polymarket sentiment tracks this: end-of-July close-above-$200 probability sits at just 43%.\nThe narrative is fragmenting. NVIDIA still owns roughly 81% of the AI chip market , but Broadcom’s custom ASICs for Alphabet and Meta are eating the story, with Bloomberg Intelligence forecasting a 27% CAGR for custom ASICs through 2033 versus 16% for AI accelerators. Broadcom guided Q3 AI semiconductor revenue to $16.0 billion, more than 200% year over year. AMD’s Meta Instinct GPU deal anchors a multi-year hyperscaler thesis , and Lisa Su noted “leading customer forecasts exceeding our initial expectations” on MI450.\nOne important number to watch is the relative size of AMD compared to NVIDIA. Right now, AMD is worth about 18% of NVIDIA’s value. At the beginning of the year, AMD was worth less than 10% NVIDIA. Investors are still piling into companies benefiting from the data center buildout, they’re just not piling into NVIDIA.\nMicron is a different animal. Samsung, SK Hynix, and Micron have avoided the aggressive oversupply that crushed memory pricing in prior cycles, a meaningful break from history. Q3 revenue exploded 345.7% year over year…",
  "statement": "Broadcom guided Q3 AI semiconductor revenue to $16.0 billion, more than 200% year over year."
 },
 {
  "id": "G100",
  "article_id": "gnews:82efe7d37ce9286b38e5aac5",
  "title": "Nvidia’s Silent AI Colonialism Is Trapping East Asia in a Fossil-Fueled Hell - The Diplomat – Asia-Pacific Current Affairs Magazine",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-02",
  "article_opening": "Every new generation of microchips uses more energy, water, and toxic chemicals than the last – with Taiwan and South Korea bearing over 90 percent of the burden.\nBy Greenpeace East Asia and Alistair Alexander\nIn late May, during Nvidia’s CEO Jensen Huang’s visit to Taiwan, local activists from the environmental group Greenpeace intercepted him to deliver a five-layer model cake reading: “AI Needs Renewable Energy.” The unexpected stunt was a reference to Huang’s own recent industry remarks, whi",
  "passage": "…y partners in the government’s “Yongin Mega-cluster” plan , a vast complex of semiconductor fabs and AI factories that will become the world’s largest industrial complex.\nShockingly, the plan calls for power from six massive LNG units (3 GW) by 2030 rather than renewables. Yet Yongin will ultimately demand about 15 GW of electricity – roughly one-sixth of South Korea’s total electricity demand – and will consume about half the water used by Seoul and its 10 million people.\nLike Taiwan, South Korea’s energy system is still mostly powered by planet-warming gas. Both countries are critically dependent on the Persian Gulf for their oil and gas, and are now facing their worst-ever energy crisis. Taiwan says it has secured supplies through the end of the year, but uncertainty looms afterward. Although South Korean President Lee Jae-myung said the country must “move very quickly toward renewable energy,” Greenpeace’s energy team recently warned that, instead of accelerating renewables, South Korea may revert to coal under the banner of “energy security” – especially as surging demand from industries like electronics intensifies.\nNow, Nvidia and the global AI frenzy are locking Taiwan and South Korea deeper into fossil fuel dependency and chronic energy insecurity. The AI boom is fast becoming a carbon trap, and it is the people in East Asia who will be unable to escape worsening pollution and runaway carbon emissions.\nWorse still, while Nvidia quietly drives this environmental d…",
  "statement": "Taiwan says it has secured supplies through the end of the year, but uncertainty looms afterward"
 },
 {
